South-west of Chamarel village, a small area of bare dunes shows sand in seven distinct shades — red, brown, violet, green, blue, purple and yellow — laid down in stripes and carved into ridges by the rain.

The colours come from volcanic basalt that weathered into clay and then into ferralitic soil: iron gives the reds, aluminium the blues and purples. Curiously, if the coloured sands are mixed, they eventually separate again into layers — a phenomenon that is still not fully understood.

The dunes have been one of the island's best-known sights since the 1960s. They are fenced off to protect them, and visitors look out over them from viewing points along the fence. The site lies within a park that also includes the main viewpoint over Chamarel Falls.

## Good to know

- The colours are most vivid in sunshine, especially in the morning or late afternoon.
- Small tubes of coloured sand are sold as souvenirs.
